> Why the extension calculation gives a base-field square root

For an odd prime p, first reduce n modulo p. Handle zero directly; for n≠0,
Euler's criterion says a root exists exactly when n^((p−1)/2)=1 modulo p.
Choose a with w=a²−n a nonresidue, so X²−w is irreducible over F_p.
In the quadratic field F_p[s]/(s²−w), multiplication is

$$
(x_1+y_1s)(x_2+y_2s)
=(x_1x_2+w y_1y_2)+(x_1y_2+x_2y_1)s.
$$
\alttext(The coefficient of one is the product of the constant coefficients plus w times the product of the s coefficients. The coefficient of s is the sum of the cross products.)

Here s is a formal field basis symbol, not the complex number i. Since
w^((p−1)/2)=−1, we have s^p=−s. Thus z=a+s has Frobenius conjugate
z^p=a−s and norm

$$
z^{p+1}=(a+s)(a-s)=a^2-w=n.
$$
\alttext(The norm of a plus s is a squared minus w, which equals the target n.)

Let r=z^((p+1)/2). Then r²=n. Moreover,
r^(p−1)=n^((p−1)/2)=1, so r^p=r. The fixed elements of Frobenius are
exactly the base field: writing r=x+ys gives x−ys=x+ys, hence y=0 because
p is odd. The resulting x is therefore the desired ordinary residue root.
The implementation checks both the vanishing y coefficient and x²=n.

Every nonzero quadratic residue has exactly two roots, r and −r: if u²=r²,
then (u−r)(u+r)=0 in a field. They are distinct for odd p. Zero has one root,
and F_2 has one root for each of its two elements. These exceptions matter to
the old report's unrestricted claim that every residue has two roots.

> The original modulus-1009 examples and root pairs

The original deterministic choices remain 439 for n=2, 162 for n=10,
999 for n=100, and 587 for n=500. Their complementary roots are 570, 847,
10 and 422. The native equation checker uses widened modular arithmetic;
the report no longer checks a potentially overflowing signed expression r*r.

The figure retains both roots of every nonzero quadratic residue modulo 1009,
but orders each pair for display: blue is the smaller root, red the larger.
That ordering is a plot convention, not a change to the solver's root choice.
The finite paired clouds reflect across y=p/2. Their appearance is not a
randomness or cryptographic-security test.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cipolla.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const std::uint64_t p=1009;
  std::print("| original n | actual unsigned root | complementary root | both equations verified |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const std::uint64_t n:{2,10,100,500}) {
    const auto r=dm::algorithms::cipolla_sqrt_result(n,p);
    const bool valid=r.exists && dm::algorithms::cipolla_root_satisfies(n,p,r.root) && dm::algorithms::cipolla_root_satisfies(n,p,r.other_root);
    if(!valid)return 1;std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,r.root,r.other_root,valid);
  }
  std::vector<double> residues,lower,upper;std::uint64_t first_nonresidue=0;
  for(std::uint64_t n=1;n<p;++n) {
    const auto r=dm::algorithms::cipolla_sqrt_result(n,p);
    if(!r.exists){if(!first_nonresidue)first_nonresidue=n;continue;}
    if(!dm::algorithms::cipolla_root_satisfies(n,p,r.root) || !dm::algorithms::cipolla_root_satisfies(n,p,r.other_root))return 1;
    residues.push_back(n);lower.push_back(std::min(r.root,r.other_root));upper.push_back(std::max(r.root,r.other_root));
  }
  if(residues.size()!=504 || first_nonresidue!=11)return 1;
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points(residues,lower,"lower",{37,99,235},2.6).points(residues,upper,"upper",{220,38,38},2.6)
      .abline_h(p/2.0,{100,116,139},1).legend({"smaller root","larger root"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
      .title("Both roots modulo 1009, ordered by value")
      .x_label("nonzero quadratic residue n").y_label("root representative")
      .x_limits(-10,1020).y_limits(-30,1200).save_svg("build/cipolla.svg");
  std::print("\nNonzero square classes: {} of {}. First positive nonresidue: {}. Both roots checked for every plotted pair.\n",residues.size(),p-1,first_nonresidue);
}
