\caption(All original root pairs remain; the complete census has 504 nonzero square classes and first nonresidue eleven.)
\alttext(Blue points are the smaller root below 504.5 and red points the complementary larger root above 504.5 for each of 504 nonzero square classes modulo 1009. A horizontal line marks their symmetry axis.)

Squaring pairs each nonzero field element x with −x, so the p−1 nonzero
elements map two-to-one onto exactly (p−1)/2 square classes. This explains
504 out of 1008; it is an exact count, not an empirical probability estimate.
Zero is deliberately excluded from this figure and count.

> Candidate density is not a guarantee about deterministic trial count

For a fixed nonzero square n, exactly (p−1)/2 auxiliary values a in F_p make
a²−n a nonresidue. A short count explains this. The equation b²=a²−n is
equivalent to (a−b)(a+b)=n. Each nonzero choice of a−b determines one pair
(a,b), because two is invertible, so there are p−1 pairs. In terms of the
quadratic character χ, this says

$$
\sum_{a\in F_p}\bigl(1+\chi(a^2-n)\bigr)=p-1,
\qquad \sum_{a\in F_p}\chi(a^2-n)=-1.
$$
\alttext(Counting solutions by their first coordinate gives p plus the sum of the quadratic characters, equal to p minus one.)

Exactly two values give a²−n=0. Combining the total count with the character
sum gives (p−1)/2 nonresidues and (p−3)/2 nonzero residues among the tested
differences. Independent uniform random auxiliary choices would therefore
succeed with probability (p−1)/(2p), for mean 2p/(p−1) trials.

This implementation instead retains the reproducible order 1,2,…,p−1,0.
The random-choice expectation is not a guarantee for that sequential order.
The table measures averages over every nonzero square input for each fixed
prime; the histogram describes that fixed-input census modulo 1009. Neither is
a runtime benchmark or a random independent-trial experiment.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cipolla_candidates.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <bit>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");std::vector<std::size_t> frequency;
  std::print("| prime | nonzero square inputs | mean actual trials | maximum trials | pair products per successful call | scalar products inside pairs |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const std::uint64_t p:{13,17,97,257,1009}) {
    std::size_t squares=0,total=0,largest=0,pair_count=0,scalar_count=0;
    for(std::uint64_t n=1;n<p;++n) {
      const auto t=dm::algorithms::cipolla_sqrt_trace(n,p);if(!t.result.exists)continue;
      ++squares;total+=t.work.candidate_tests;largest=std::max(largest,t.work.candidate_tests);
      pair_count=t.work.extension_result_products+t.work.extension_base_squares;scalar_count=t.work.extension_scalar_products;
      if(pair_count!=std::bit_width(p/2+1)+std::popcount(p/2+1) || scalar_count!=5*pair_count)return 1;
      if(p==1009){if(frequency.size()<=t.work.candidate_tests)frequency.resize(t.work.candidate_tests+1);++frequency[t.work.candidate_tests];}
    }
    if(squares!=(p-1)/2)return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.6f} | {} | {} | {} |\n",p,squares,double(total)/squares,largest,pair_count,scalar_count);
  }
  std::vector<double> trials,counts;std::size_t total=0;
  for(std::size_t k=1;k<frequency.size();++k){trials.push_back(k);counts.push_back(frequency[k]);total+=frequency[k];}
  if(total!=504)return 1;
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.bars(trials,counts,"fixed census",{37,99,235}).legend({"504 fixed square inputs"},{{37,99,235}})
      .title("Deterministic auxiliary work modulo 1009")
      .x_label("actual candidates tested before success").y_label("number of square inputs")
      .x_limits(0.5,frequency.size()-0.5).y_limits(0,1.25*(*std::max_element(counts.begin(),counts.end())))
      .save_svg("build/cipolla_candidates.svg");
}
```
