| 257 | 128 | 1.898438 | 6 | 10 | 50 |
| 1009 | 504 | 2.051587 | 10 | 16 | 80 |
\image(figures/cipolla_candidates.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual deterministic search lengths vary with the input; extension powering work depends only on the prime.)
\alttext(A histogram counts how many of the 504 nonzero square inputs modulo 1009 require each actual number of auxiliary tests. It measures a complete fixed census, not random-trial probabilities or seconds.)

For exponent E=(p+1)/2, the retained right-to-left loop performs popcount(E)
result multiplications and bit_width(E) base squares. The latter includes the
last unused square. Each pair operation performs five reduced scalar products;
it does not optimise away products with zero coefficients. Scalar Euler powers
also retain their closing square. These details explain the reported actual
counts, rather than substituting counts for a different optimised loop.

For T actual candidates, the solver performs O((T+1) log p) named modular
products in a fixed-word model, including the bounded deterministic prime
validation. Sequential search can inspect up to p candidates; a positive
candidate cap bounds that work. The ordinary call retains constant numeric
state; a trace owns O(T+log p) records. The counters exclude primality's
internal products and are not an arbitrary-precision bit-cost model. No
throughput comparison with another square-root method is inferred.

> The missing-zero case and the full unsigned range

Modulo 3, the nonzero square 1 exposes a bug in a search restricted to
auxiliaries 1 and 2: both give w=0. The only suitable auxiliary is a=0,
where w=2. The revised order reaches it after those two failures and produces
the valid root 2, with complementary root 1. Zero differences are rejected as
auxiliaries rather than mistaken for a quadratic field.

The legacy return type is signed long long, while the input prime can occupy
all of uint64. A raw computed root above LLONG_MAX cannot be cast safely into
that interface. One complementary root is at most p/2, so the legacy call now
uses the other root when necessary. The unsigned-result API retains the
actual raw root, its complementary root and the selected legacy representative.
The original small-prime choices remain intact.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto small=dm::algorithms::cipolla_sqrt_trace(1,3);
  if(small.result.root!=2 || small.candidates.size()!=3)return 1;
  std::print("| actual auxiliary mod 3 | w | Euler value |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& c:small.candidates)std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",c.a,c.w,c.euler_value);
  std::print("\nCorrected sqrt(1) mod 3: {} and {}; legacy value {}.\n",small.result.root,small.result.other_root,small.result.legacy_root);
  constexpr std::uint64_t p=18446744073709551557ULL;
  const auto large=dm::algorithms::cipolla_sqrt_result(9,p);
  const auto signed_limit=static_cast<std::uint64_t>(std::numeric_limits<long long>::max());
  if(!large.exists || large.root<=signed_limit || large.legacy_root!=3)return 1;
  std::print("Full-range prime {}: raw root {}, other root {}, safe legacy root {}.\n",p,large.root,large.other_root,large.legacy_root);
  std::print("Both unsigned roots satisfy the equation: {} and {}.\n",dm::algorithms::cipolla_root_satisfies(9,p,large.root),dm::algorithms::cipolla_root_satisfies(9,p,large.other_root));
  const auto zero=dm::algorithms::cipolla_sqrt_result(13,13);
  const auto binary=dm::algorithms::cipolla_sqrt_trace(std::numeric_limits<std::uint64_t>::max(),2);
  std::print("Zero class modulo 13: root {}, other {} (one distinct root).\n",zero.root,zero.other_root);
  std::print("UINT64_MAX modulo 2 normalizes to {}, with unique root {}.\n",binary.normalized_n,binary.result.root);
}
