\caption(Exact caps pass; lower caps and invalid moduli reject explicitly. The worked example uses 48 named scalar products outside prime validation.)

The variable candidate search and exponent-bit branches expose variable work;
this routine makes no secret-independent timing claim. Equation verification
certifies the modular root for a validated prime, not the ordering of the root
pair or any property of a cryptographic protocol. All returned records own
their values and calls share no mutable state.

Fourteen new native tests enumerate every residue in the 55 prime fields through
257, totalling 6,338 residue inputs, and check normalized aliases against explicit
square sets. They retain the original four roots and all 1009 residue verdicts.
A separate add-and-double modular arithmetic oracle verifies every recorded
candidate and pair transition, independently of the core's unsigned-128 products.
Another 1,000 known squares span four large primes through 18446744073709551557,
including raw roots above the signed range and their safe legacy complements.
Signed/unsigned endpoints, pseudoprime/composite rejection, the missing-zero
case, exact/one-below caps, closing-square accounting, shortcut paths and owned
copies pass. All fourteen cases also pass AddressSanitizer and UBSan; existing
arithmetic regressions remain in the native runner.

No observational dataset is needed for these exact finite-field identities.
The report's complete census describes a particular prime and deterministic
search order. It does not establish a universal trial count or a performance
ranking.

> References

- Michele Cipolla (1903), *Un metodo per la risoluzione della congruenza di
  secondo grado*. The attribution and year are recorded in the notes to
  section 3.5 of the following reference, correcting the old report's 1907 date.
- A. Menezes, P. van Oorschot and S. Vanstone (1996), *Handbook of Applied
  Cryptography*, Algorithm 3.39 and section 3.12 notes.
  https://cacr.uwaterloo.ca/hac/about/chap3.pdf
  Its irreducible polynomial X²−bX+n becomes this representation under b=2a:
  the discriminant is 4(a²−n), with the same quadratic character as a²−n.
  This implementation uses deterministic candidate order in place of random b.
